					<div id="dsq-comment-body-132" class="dsq-comment-body">
						<div id="dsq-comment-message-132" class="dsq-comment-message"><p>Can you clarify the purpose of the soapbox? Is it for people to talk about the issues they think are most important? What sort of things are appropriate for the soapbox portion and what sort of things are appropriate once you start digging into feedback?</p>
</div>
					</div>
				</li>
					<li id="dsq-comment-133">
					<div id="dsq-comment-header-133" class="dsq-comment-header">
						<cite id="dsq-cite-133">
	http://blog.cromwellhaus.com							<span id="dsq-author-user-133">cromwellryan</span>
							</cite>
					</div>
					<div id="dsq-comment-body-133" class="dsq-comment-body">
						<div id="dsq-comment-message-133" class="dsq-comment-message"><p>The soapbox is entirely open.  People can talk about the weather if they choose, but they might not be envited back <img src='http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/wp-includes/images/smilies/icon_smile.gif' alt=':)' class='wp-smiley' /> .  The benefit I find is two fold: let people get things off their chest that they are passionate about such that they don&#8217;t feel compelled to inject tangential comments into the natural flow of discussion.  Second, we&#8217;ve found it helps get open dialog flowing.</p>
<p>I&#8217;m sure there are benefits and negatives to this process, but timeboxing it is very helpful I find.</p>
